.TH ls "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ls \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBls [\-acdfgilrstu] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ls -- list directory contents
Usage:	ls [ -acdfgilrstu ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-a	List all files (including '.' and '..')
	-c	Use attribute change instead of modified time for '-l' and '-t'
	-d	Treat directories like files
	-f	Treat file as a directory even if it is not (implies '-a')
	-g	Display group owner, rather than owner
	-i	Print the i-number as well
	-l	Longer listing format
	-r	Reverse the order of all sorting
	-s	Print the file size in blocks as well
	-t	Sort by times, normally newest first (default: sort by name)
	-u	Use accessed rather than modified time
.HE
.B ls
prints information about each named
.IR file .
By default,
.B ls
sorts by file name and prints only the name of each
.IR file .
If a
.I file
is a directory,
.B ls
sorts and lists its contents (not including
.B .
and
.BR .. ,
unless
.B \-a
is given) rather than the directory's own name; the argument list
itself is always sorted and shown in full first, and each directory's
contents follow, headed by the directory's name if more than one
argument was given.
If no
.I file
is named, the current directory is listed.
.IP \fB\-a\fR
List all entries, including
.B .
and
.BR .. .
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Show (and, with
.BR \-t ,
sort by) the time the file's attributes were last changed (its ctime),
instead of the modification time.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Treat directories as plain files (list their own entry, not their
contents).
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Force each argument to be treated as a directory (implies
.BR \-a ,
and turns off the
\fBl\fR, \fBr\fR, \fBs\fR, and \fBt\fR
options and all sorting).
.IP \fB\-g\fR
In the long format, print the group owner instead of the file's
owner.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Print the i-number of each file.
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Long format: mode bits, link count, owner (or group, with
.BR \-g ),
size in bytes (or major/minor device numbers for special files), time,
and name.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Reverse the sense of the sort.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Print the size in blocks of each file.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Sort by time, newest first, instead of by name.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Sort by, and with
.B \-l
print, the access time instead of the modification time.
.PP
When more than one of
.BR \-c ", " \-u ,
and the default apply, the most recently given takes effect (they set
the same internal selector).
.PP
The mode field printed by
.B \-l
begins with one of the following:
.DS
.ta 8n 30n
	\fB\-\fR	regular file
	\fBb\fR	block special file
	\fBc\fR	character special file
	\fBd\fR	directory
	\fBp\fR	pipe
	\fBm\fR	multiplexed (multiplexor) file
	\fBx\fR	other/unknown
.DE
followed by nine permission characters in three groups of three
(owner, group, other):
.DS
.ta 8n 30n
	\fBr\fR	readable
	\fBw\fR	writable
	\fBx\fR	executable (or \fBs\fR, if set-user/group-ID is also set)
	\fB\-\fR	permission not given
.DE
and a final character that is
.B t
if the sticky bit is set on the file, or a blank otherwise.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR lc ,
.B chmod
